A straightforward version is to test every node as a possible subtree root. For each node, the helper checks the BST range and counts its nodes. If the subtree is valid, its count can update the answer.

This is easy to follow, but the same nodes may be visited many times, so it is slower than a bottom-up solution. The code does not change the tree.

```cpp
#include <climits>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
    bool isBST(TreeNode* node, long long low, long long high, int& count) {
        if (node == nullptr) {
            return true;
        }

        if (node->data <= low || node->data >= high) {
            return false;
        }

        int leftCount = 0;
        int rightCount = 0;
        bool leftValid = isBST(node->left, low, node->data, leftCount);
        bool rightValid = isBST(node->right, node->data, high, rightCount);

        if (!leftValid || !rightValid) {
            return false;
        }

        count = leftCount + rightCount + 1;
        return true;
    }

public:
    int largestBST(TreeNode* root) {
        if (root == nullptr) {
            return 0;
        }

        int answer = 0;
        int currentCount = 0;

        if (isBST(root, LLONG_MIN, LLONG_MAX, currentCount)) {
            answer = currentCount;
        }

        answer = max(answer, largestBST(root->left));
        answer = max(answer, largestBST(root->right));
        return answer;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: O(n^2) in the worst case, because many subtrees are checked again.
Space complexity: O(h) for recursion, where h is the tree height.

Postorder traversal gives the child results before the parent is checked. Each result stores whether the subtree is a BST, its smallest value, its largest value, and its size.

For a valid subtree, the largest value on the left must be smaller than the current node, and the smallest value on the right must be larger. A null child uses opposite integer limits, which makes a leaf work without a special case.

```java
class Solution {
    private static class Info {
        boolean isBST;
        int minValue;
        int maxValue;
        int size;

        Info(boolean isBST, int minValue, int maxValue, int size) {
            this.isBST = isBST;
            this.minValue = minValue;
            this.maxValue = maxValue;
            this.size = size;
        }
    }

    public int largestBST(TreeNode root) {
        return findInfo(root).bestSize;
    }

    private Result findInfo(TreeNode node) {
        if (node == null) {
            return new Result(true, Integer.MAX_VALUE, Integer.MIN_VALUE, 0, 0);
        }

        Result left = findInfo(node.left);
        Result right = findInfo(node.right);
        int best = Math.max(left.bestSize, right.bestSize);

        if (left.isBST && right.isBST
                && left.maxValue < node.data
                && node.data < right.minValue) {
            int size = left.size + right.size + 1;
            int minValue = Math.min(node.data, left.minValue);
            int maxValue = Math.max(node.data, right.maxValue);
            best = Math.max(best, size);
            return new Result(true, minValue, maxValue, size, best);
        }

        return new Result(false, Integer.MIN_VALUE, Integer.MAX_VALUE, 0, best);
    }

    private static class Result {
        boolean isBST;
        int minValue;
        int maxValue;
        int size;
        int bestSize;

        Result(boolean isBST, int minValue, int maxValue, int size, int bestSize) {
            this.isBST = isBST;
            this.minValue = minValue;
            this.maxValue = maxValue;
            this.size = size;
            this.bestSize = bestSize;
        }
    }
}
```

Dry run: for a leaf with value 7, both children return empty-BST information. The checks pass, so the leaf has size 1. Its minimum and maximum are both 7, and that information moves to its parent.

Time complexity: O(n), since every node is processed once.
Space complexity: O(h) for the recursive call stack.

Here the tree is scanned from each possible root, but the validation uses lower and upper bounds instead of only comparing direct children. That catches values hidden deeper in a subtree.

The helper returns the number of nodes when the whole subtree is valid, and -1 when it is not. Since the answer is based on size, negative node values do not need any special treatment.

```go
package solution

import "math"

func LargestBST(root *TreeNode) int {
    if root == nil {
        return 0
    }

    best := 0
    if size, ok := validSubtree(root, math.MinInt64, math.MaxInt64); ok {
        best = size
    }

    leftBest := LargestBST(root.Left)
    rightBest := LargestBST(root.Right)
    if leftBest > best {
        best = leftBest
    }
    if rightBest > best {
        best = rightBest
    }
    return best
}

func validSubtree(node *TreeNode, low int64, high int64) (int, bool) {
    if node == nil {
        return 0, true
    }

    value := int64(node.Data)
    if value <= low || value >= high {
        return 0, false
    }

    leftSize, leftOK := validSubtree(node.Left, low, value)
    rightSize, rightOK := validSubtree(node.Right, value, high)
    if !leftOK || !rightOK {
        return 0, false
    }

    return leftSize + rightSize + 1, true
}
```

Dry run: suppose a node has value 5 and a value 3 appears somewhere in its right subtree. The right recursion receives the range `(5, high)`, so 3 fails immediately. That whole subtree is rejected even if its direct child looked acceptable.

This is useful as a simple checking method, but it repeats work. A skewed tree can cause O(n^2) time. The recursion depth is O(h), where h is the height.

The recursive function returns all the information needed by its parent. A valid result contains its size and value range. If a subtree fails, its range is replaced with values that make any parent check fail.

The answer is updated whenever a valid subtree is found. For an empty tree the method returns 0, and duplicate values are rejected because the comparisons are strict.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    private class State {
        public bool IsBST;
        public int Minimum;
        public int Maximum;
        public int Size;

        public State(bool isBST, int minimum, int maximum, int size) {
            IsBST = isBST;
            Minimum = minimum;
            Maximum = maximum;
            Size = size;
        }
    }

    private int best;

    public int largestBST(TreeNode root) {
        best = 0;
        Visit(root);
        return best;
    }

    private State Visit(TreeNode node) {
        if (node == null) {
            return new State(true, int.MaxValue, int.MinValue, 0);
        }

        State left = Visit(node.left);
        State right = Visit(node.right);

        bool valid = left.IsBST
            && right.IsBST
            && left.Maximum < node.data
            && node.data < right.Minimum;

        if (!valid) {
            return new State(false, int.MinValue, int.MaxValue, 0);
        }

        int size = left.Size + right.Size + 1;
        best = Math.Max(best, size);

        int minimum = Math.Min(node.data, left.Minimum);
        int maximum = Math.Max(node.data, right.Maximum);
        return new State(true, minimum, maximum, size);
    }
}
```

One edge case is a tree where the root is invalid but one child is a valid BST. The invalid result does not erase `best`, so the child's size is still preserved. A single node also works because the empty-child limits pass both comparisons.

The input links are only read, not changed.

Time complexity: O(n), with constant work at each node.
Space complexity: O(h) for recursion, plus one small state object per active call.

## Solution
```java
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null, right = null }
 * }
 **/

class Solution {
        final int MIN_INDEX=0;
        final int MAX_INDEX=1;
        final int COUNT_INDEX=2;
        public int largestBST(TreeNode root) {
            return largestBSTSolution(root)[COUNT_INDEX];
        }

        int[] largestBSTSolution(TreeNode root){
            if(root == null){
                return new int[]{Integer.MAX_VALUE, Integer.MIN_VALUE, 0};
            }

            int[] left = largestBSTSolution(root.left);
            int[] right = largestBSTSolution(root.right);

            if(root.data > left[MAX_INDEX] && root.data < right[MIN_INDEX]){
                return new int[]{Math.min(left[MIN_INDEX], root.data), Math.max(root.data, right[MAX_INDEX]), left[COUNT_INDEX]+right[COUNT_INDEX]+1};
            }

            return new int[]{Integer.MIN_VALUE, Integer.MAX_VALUE, Math.max(left[COUNT_INDEX], right[COUNT_INDEX])};
        }
}
```

